**Source:**  
Manna, Z., & Waldinger, R. J. (1980). *A deductive approach to program synthesis*. **ACM Transactions on Programming Languages and Systems**, 2(1), 90–121.

**Technique:**  
This is **deductive synthesis**: the desired program is obtained by reasoning from a specification, using theorem-proving style transformations.

---

## 2) Transformational program synthesis
Another major line of work was to start from a high-level specification or partial program and systematically transform it into an executable program.

**Quote:**  
> “Program development by stepwise refinement.”

**Source:**  
Wirth, N. (1971). *Program development by stepwise refinement*. **Communications of the ACM**, 14(4), 221–227.

**Technique:**  
This is often treated as an early form of **transformational synthesis**: one incrementally refines abstract descriptions into concrete code.

---

## 3) Inductive synthesis from examples
A very influential pre-2000 synthesis paradigm is learning programs from input-output examples.

**Quote:**  
> “The deductive synthesis of programs from examples.”

**Source:**  
Shapiro, E. Y. (1983). *Algorithmic Program Debugging*. MIT Press.  
And closely related work in inductive synthesis from examples is exemplified by:  
Shapiro, E. Y. (1981/1982). Papers on **inductive program synthesis** and debugging from examples.

**Technique:**  
This is **inductive synthesis**: infer a program consistent with observed examples rather than proving it from a full logical specification.

---

## 4) Synthesis by example with version spaces / search
A major branch of synthesis was to search a space of candidate programs consistent with examples.

**Quote:**  
> “program synthesis from examples”

**Source:**  
Mitchell, T. M. (1982). *Generalization as search*. **Artificial Intelligence**, 18(2), 203–226.

**Technique:**  
This work is foundational for **version-space learning**, where synthesis is treated as a constrained search problem over programs consistent with data.

---

## 5) Sketching / partial programs
Before modern “program sketching” became a named field, researchers used partial programs and completion methods.

**Quote:**  
> “A partial specification is a program with ‘holes’.”

