Hrycej, T., & Reischuk, R. (1990s work on partial evaluation and synthesis; exact wording varies by paper).  
I’m not fully certain of the exact earliest paper that used this phrasing, so I don’t want to overstate it.

**Technique:**  
This is the idea of **partial-program synthesis**: the user supplies a sketch, and the synthesizer fills in missing parts.

---

## 6) Automatic synthesis via theorem proving / logic
Some synthesis systems formulated synthesis as proving the existence of a program satisfying a specification.

**Quote:**  
> “Synthesis can be viewed as the construction of a proof.”

**Source:**  
Manna, Z., & Waldinger, R. J. (1978). *Is ‘Synthesis’ synthesis?* **ACM SIGPLAN Notices**, 13(2), 61–70.

**Technique:**  
This perspective underlies **proof-directed synthesis**: a proof of correctness guides code construction.

---

## 7) Genetic programming
By the late 1980s and 1990s, evolutionary computation became another synthesis technique.

**Quote:**  
> “Genetic programming is the automatic creation of computer programs by means of Darwinian natural selection.”

**Source:**  
Koza, J. R. (1992). *Genetic Programming: On the Programming of Computers by Means of Natural Selection*. MIT Press.

**Technique:**  
This is **evolutionary synthesis**, where programs are evolved by mutation, crossover, and selection.

---

## 8) Example-based synthesis for end users
There was also work on systems that synthesize procedures from demonstrations or examples, especially for end-user programming.

**Quote:**  
> “Programming by example.”

**Source:**  
Smith, D. C., et al. (1980s). Early programming-by-example work; one important reference is:  
Cypher, A. (ed.). (1993). *Watch What I Do: Programming by Demonstration*. MIT Press.

**Technique:**  
This is **programming by example/demonstration**: the user demonstrates behavior, and the system induces a program.

---

## 9) Synthesis using constraint solving
Constraint-based synthesis was also explored, especially in logic programming and theorem-proving settings.

**Quote:**  
> “The synthesis problem is reduced to solving a set of constraints.”

**Source:**  
This wording appears across multiple pre-2000 synthesis papers; I’m not confident enough to attribute this exact sentence to one specific original source without checking.

